+# Read sources / limits
+
+The three required fixed R2 reports H15, H16 and H17 were fetched through GitHub
+and read in full from immutable source artifact10899042151. They are prior
+negative results, not R3 performance measurements. H15 documented original fault
+and individual-group regressions; H16 documented long-coast and distance failures;
+H17 found group-dependent preferences, not a universal physical delay.
+
+Runtime core/readout/Timeline, canonical launch/champion_v8 YAML, official
+score/match/distance functions, v6 aggregation, guarded fault_windows, H11
+common_fault_windows/inject_common and frozen split were read. Sixty baseline
+source/test/scorer/role files are pinned in BASELINE_PINS.json. The low-speed
+anchor is the exact source of tools/research_lock/low_speed.py at6190e82
+(blob674666746d1f6c20bd6d27e4467fd9f246e8c5dc), read through GitHub; its old loader
+role is not reused. The anchor logic is reproduced verbatim for development.
+
+Scientific context: Herbster and Warmuth, Tracking the Best Linear Predictor,
+JMLR1(Sep),281-309(2001), https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v1/herbster01a.html.
+Read publisher HTML abstract only, not full text. It distinguishes tracking and
+fixed-comparator losses in online prediction. Our capped eight-record heuristic
+and physical fallback are engineering assumptions; no theorem from the article
+is transferred to this nonlinear odometry problem.
+
+Consensus and Scite were NOT called again: task and prior session already
+reported monthly search/MCP stops30/25. No fresh search, fulltext, citation
+context or quota-status verification is claimed. Public primary-source abstract
+and actual Wolfram algebra are sufficient context for executing the experiment,
+not evidence of better odometry. Failed auxiliary author-page request is not used.
+
+MATH.wl contains the exact evaluated Wolfram code; MATH_OUTPUT.txt transcribes
+actual output, assumptions and warnings. No model change or parameter choice
+uses validation, final data or another R3 validation result.
